\title{Phase Locking and Spectral Obstructions in\\Complex-Weighted Undirected Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze an undirected Bernoulli graph in which each present edge receives one fixed complex amplitude $z$. The Bernoulli parameter $p\in[0,1]$ remains real and governs edge presence; $z$ is an edge weight, not a probability. For every finite realization, the weighted adjacency matrix factors as $A_z=zB$, where $B$ is the real symmetric zero--one adjacency matrix. This identity obstructs the proposed circular spectral law: $A_z$ is normal, its adjoint is $A_{\bar z}$, and every eigenvalue is a real eigenvalue of $B$ multiplied by $z$. Thus the spectrum lies on the line $z\mathbb R$, before and after centering. We prove a deterministic row-sum bound, establish exact scaling of expected weighted subgraph counts, and use the complete graph on four vertices to refute a universal radius $|z|\sqrt n$. Algorithms and corrected directed models are also discussed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi model $G(n,p)$ independently places an edge between each unordered pair of $n$ vertices with probability $p$. Its adjacency matrix is real symmetric. A tempting complex extension gives each present edge a common amplitude $z\in\mathbb C$. Because the entries are then $0$ or $z$, one might compare this matrix with a non-Hermitian independent-entry ensemble and predict a circular cloud of eigenvalues of radius approximately $|z|\sqrt n$.

This analogy overlooks perfect transpose correlation and common phase. Opposite off-diagonal entries are identical, and all nonzero entries share one scalar factor. The resulting matrix is a complex multiple of a real symmetric matrix. Its phase rotates all eigenvalues together and cannot produce angular dispersion.

We distinguish edge probability $p\in[0,1]$, complex edge amplitude $z$, and matrix symmetry. Our results are finite and deterministic once the graph is realized. They show exact phase locking, normality, row-sum control, and a finite obstruction to the proposed square-root radius. They also identify direction and centering as separate changes required for a plausible circular-law model.

\section{Model and definitions}
Let $V=\{1,\dots,n\}$ and let $E_0$ be the unordered pairs of distinct vertices. For each $e\in E_0$, choose an independent Bernoulli variable $X_e$ with $\mathbb P(X_e=1)=p$. The loopless indicator adjacency matrix $B$ is
\[
 B_{ij}=\begin{cases}X_{\{i,j\}},&i\ne j,\\0,&i=j.\end{cases}
\]
It satisfies $B=B^{\mathsf T}=B^*$.

\begin{definition}[Fixed-amplitude complex graph]
For $z\in\mathbb C$, the fixed-amplitude weighted adjacency matrix is
\[
 (A_z)_{ij}=\begin{cases}z,&B_{ij}=1,\\0,&B_{ij}=0.\end{cases}
\]
Here $p$ is a probability and $z$ is an amplitude.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Phase line]
For $z\in\mathbb C$, let $L_z=z\mathbb R=\{zt:t\in\mathbb R\}$. For nonzero $z$, this is the line through the origin at angle $\arg z$ modulo $\pi$.
\end{definition}

\section{Exact matrix structure}
\begin{theorem}[Scalar Factorization]
For every finite undirected graph and every $z\in\mathbb C$,
\[
 A_z=zB.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If an edge is absent, the corresponding entry on each side is zero. If it is present, $B_{ij}=1$ and both entries are $z$. Thus the matrices agree entrywise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Adjoint Phase Relation]
For every undirected realization, $A_z^*=A_{\bar z}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $B^*=B$, one has $(zB)^*=\bar zB=A_{\bar z}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Normality]
Every fixed-amplitude complex adjacency matrix of an undirected graph is normal:
\[
 A_zA_z^*=A_z^*A_z.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both products equal $|z|^2B^2$.
\end{proof}

Normality gives unitary diagonalizability and, outside the spectrum, the resolvent formula
\[
 \|(wI-A_z)^{-1}\|_2=\frac1{\operatorname{dist}(w,\operatorname{spec}(A_z))}.
\]
Normality alone does not force a spectrum onto a line; the scalar--symmetric factorization is the stronger fact that does so here.

\section{Spectral phase locking}
\begin{theorem}[Eigenpair Transport]
If $Bv=\mu v$ with $v\ne0$, then $A_zv=(z\mu)v$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By factorization, $A_zv=zBv=z\mu v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Eigenpair Pullback]
If $z\ne0$ and $A_zv=\lambda v$ with $v\ne0$, then $Bv=(\lambda/z)v$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Divide $zBv=\lambda v$ by $z$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Phase-Locking Theorem]
For every fixed-amplitude complex graph,
\[
 \operatorname{spec}(A_z)=z\operatorname{spec}(B)\subseteq L_z.
\]
For $z\ne0$, algebraic multiplicities are preserved under $\mu\mapsto z\mu$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The real symmetric spectral theorem supplies an orthonormal eigenbasis of $B$ with real eigenvalues. Transporting that basis gives all eigenpairs of $A_z$.
\end{proof}

Writing $z=|z|e^{i\theta}$, every weighted eigenvalue obeys $e^{-i\theta}\lambda\in\mathbb R$, equivalently
\[
 \Im(\lambda\bar z)=0.
\]

\begin{corollary}[No Circular Empirical Limit]
For nonzero fixed $z$ and any positive real normalization $a_n$, every empirical spectral measure
\[
 \nu_n=\frac1n\sum_{k=1}^n\delta_{\lambda_k(A_z)/a_n}
\]
is supported on $L_z$. Any weak limit is supported on that line and therefore cannot be uniform on a disk of positive area.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Positive real scaling preserves the closed set $L_z$. Weak limits of probability measures supported on a fixed closed set remain supported there. A uniform disk measure assigns zero mass to a line.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Centering Preserves Phase Locking]
If $M=\mathbb E[B]$, then
\[
 A_z-\mathbb E[A_z]=z(B-M),
\]
and the centered matrix is normal with spectrum in $L_z$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Linearity gives $\mathbb E[A_z]=zM$. The matrix $B-M$ is real symmetric, so the preceding theorems apply.
\end{proof}

Centering may remove a large mean eigenvalue, but it cannot remove transpose symmetry.

\section{Deterministic radial bounds}
\begin{lemma}[Row-Sum Eigenvalue Bound]
Let $M\in\mathbb R^{n\times n}$ satisfy $\sum_j|M_{ij}|\le R$ for every $i$. If $Mv=\mu v$ and $v\ne0$, then $|\mu|\le R$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose $i$ with $|v_i|=\max_j|v_j|>0$. Then
\[
 |\mu||v_i|=\left|\sum_jM_{ij}v_j\right|
 \le\sum_j|M_{ij}||v_j|\le R|v_i|.
\]
Cancel $|v_i|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Scaled Row-Sum Bound]
Under the lemma's assumptions, every transported eigenvalue satisfies
\[
 |z\mu|\le |z|R.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use $|z\mu|=|z||\mu|$ and the lemma.
\end{proof}

For a simple graph, $R$ may be chosen as the maximum degree $\Delta(G)$. Hence
\[
 \operatorname{spec}(A_z)\subseteq L_z\cap\{w:|w|\le |z|\Delta(G)\}.
\]
This disk is merely an outer envelope; the spectrum remains on a line.

\section{The four-vertex obstruction}
\begin{theorem}[Complete Four-Vertex Eigenpair]
For $K_4$ and $\mathbf1=(1,1,1,1)^{\mathsf T}$,
\[
 A_z\mathbf1=3z\mathbf1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every row of the unweighted adjacency matrix sums to three, so $B\mathbf1=3\mathbf1$. Transport the eigenpair.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Square-Root-Disk Obstruction]
If $z\ne0$, the weighted adjacency matrix of $K_4$ has an eigenvalue outside the disk of radius $|z|\sqrt4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding eigenvalue $3z$ has modulus $3|z|>2|z|=|z|\sqrt4$.
\end{proof}

More generally, $K_n$ has weighted spectrum $\{(n-1)z,-z,\dots,-z\}$. The leading eigenvalue is linear in $n$. For the loopless random graph,
\[
 \mathbb E[B]=p(J-I),
\]
whose all-ones eigenvalue is $p(n-1)$. Thus an uncentered dense model naturally contains a mean direction of order $pn$, while centered fluctuations occur on a square-root scale. This outlier issue is independent of line confinement.

\section{Expected weighted subgraph counts}
Let $S_1,\dots,S_m\subseteq E_0$ be prescribed edge sets and define
\[
 N_S(G)=\sum_{r=1}^m\mathbf1_{\{S_r\subseteq G\}}.
\]
The sets may overlap and need not be distinct.

\begin{lemma}[Pattern Occurrence]
For every $r$, $\mathbb P(S_r\subseteq G)=p^{|S_r|}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
All required edges must occur, and their Bernoulli indicators are independent.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Weighted Subgraph Expectation]
For every $z\in\mathbb C$,
\[
 \mathbb E[zN_S]=z\sum_{r=1}^m p^{|S_r|}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By linearity of expectation and the lemma,
\[
 \mathbb E[N_S]=\sum_r\mathbb E[\mathbf1_{\{S_r\subseteq G\}}]
 =\sum_rp^{|S_r|}.
\]
Multiplication by fixed $z$ commutes with the finite expectation.
\end{proof}

Again, common amplitude rotates and dilates a real first moment rather than creating two-dimensional phase statistics.

\section{Algorithms and numerical diagnostics}
To sample the model, draw independent Bernoulli variables only for $i<j$, reflect them across the diagonal, and set the diagonal to zero. Dense construction costs $O(n^2)$ time and memory. A full symmetric eigendecomposition costs $O(n^3)$ time and $O(n^2)$ memory. Computing the real eigenvalues $\mu_k$ of $B$ and returning $z\mu_k$ is more efficient and more accurate than applying a general complex eigensolver to $A_z$.

For $z\ne0$, define the normalized phase residual
\[
 r(\lambda;z)=\frac{|\Im(\lambda\bar z)|}{|z|\max(1,|\lambda|)}.
\]
The theory predicts zero; computed values should be near floating-point precision. A radial diagnostic is
\[
 q_{\sqrt n}=\frac{\max_k|\lambda_k|}{|z|\sqrt n}.
\]
A value above one refutes containment for that realization. A value below one proves only containment and gives no evidence of circular filling. Angular residual, rather than radius alone, is decisive.

At $n=1000$, $p=\log(n)/n$, and $z=0.5+0.3i$, a scatter plot will place all computed eigenvalues on $z\mathbb R$ up to roundoff. The sample may lie inside the proposed square-root disk because the leading sparse mean scale $|z|\log n$ is then smaller than $|z|\sqrt n$, but such containment does not support a circular law.

\section{Comparison with candidate ensembles}
A circular law is plausible for centered non-Hermitian matrices with sufficiently independent entries. A corrected directed model independently chooses each ordered edge and sets
\[
 C_{ij}=z(X_{ij}-p),\qquad i\ne j,
\]
with zero diagonal. Normalization by $|z|\sqrt{np(1-p)}$ removes scale. Direction breaks transpose locking; centering removes the rank-one mean component.

A different modification assigns independent unit phases to undirected edges and places conjugate weights across the diagonal. The resulting magnetic adjacency matrix is Hermitian, so its spectrum is real. Under suitable moments, semicircular rather than circular behavior is the natural conjecture. Thus complex entries do not determine spectral dimension; adjoint symmetry does.

These distinctions matter in wave networks, oscillator systems, and complex-valued signal models. A common complex gain rotates all modal eigenvalues while preserving eigenspaces. Heterogeneous phases, delays, or directed couplings are needed for genuinely phase-selective behavior.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The original circular-law proposal has two independent defects. Undirected symmetry confines every eigenvalue to one line, and lack of centering permits a linear-scale mean outlier. The minimal corrected program is therefore to use directed independent edges and subtract their mean.

For fixed $0<p<1$, the centered directed model normalized by $|z|\sqrt{np(1-p)}$ is a natural candidate for convergence to the uniform disk. In sparse directed models with $p=p_n\to0$, a logarithmic threshold is expected: below it, isolated rows create zero eigenvalues and singularity. For independently phased Hermitian graphs, a semicircle limit is the more appropriate target. Uncentered directed models should exhibit one eigenvalue near $zpn$ and a square-root-scale bulk. Finally, quantitative pseudospectral comparisons can distinguish the exact stability of the normal undirected model from the sensitivity of nonnormal directed matrices.

\section{Conclusion}
For every undirected realization with one common complex amplitude,
\[
 A_z=zB
\]
with $B$ real symmetric. Consequently $A_z$ is normal, its adjoint conjugates only the amplitude, its eigenvectors agree with those of $B$, and its spectrum lies on $z\mathbb R$. Row-sum bounds scale by $|z|$, expected weighted counts scale by $z$, and $K_4$ already disproves a universal $|z|\sqrt n$ radius for uncentered realizations.

Global phase is spectrally rigid. Two-dimensional spectral behavior requires genuinely non-Hermitian independence, while centering is needed to remove the mean outlier. The obstruction therefore does more than reject a conjecture: it identifies the assumptions required for the correct replacement.

\end{document}

